WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.640
At two hours and 15 minutes, Bohemian Rhapsody
is certainly not a short movie, but there

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.360
are still a stack of amazing songs and scenes
that were cut from the final film.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.730
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video I'm revealing and explaining

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:17.550
seven cool scenes and songs deleted from the
new Queen biopic!

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.940
Keep watching to the end to find out why the
filmmakers cut some of the epic Live Aid finale

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.220
and make sure you subscribe and also leave
a comment about the movie for a chance to

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.670
win one of these cool Bohemian Rhapsody merch
packs.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:32.540
While early scenes in the movie show us how
Freddie Mercury first met and formed a band

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.630
with guitarist Brian May and drummer Roger
Taylor, there was another scene filmed that

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:41.370
we never got to see and that was how bass
guitarist John Deacon became part of the group.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.989
Tim Staffell was Smile's original lead singer
and bassist, and in the film he quits the

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:48.540
band the same night that Freddie meets Brian
and Roger.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.530
There's a line where Brian asks Freddie if
he also plays the bass, which he doesn't,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.359
and then there was meant to be a follow-up
scene showing how the band found their new

00:00:56.359 --> 00:00:57.399
bassist, John Deacon.

00:00:57.399 --> 00:01:02.289
Actor Joe Mazzello, who plays Deacon, says
they shot a proper introduction for his character,

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:05.860
where he was approached by Queen to join the
band while he was at work soldering.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.799
I can see why the scene ended up getting scrapped
in order to keep the story moving forward,

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:14.640
but Mazzello says he wished the scene had
made it into the film as he feels it shows

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.409
what an "accidental rock star" Deacon was.

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:21.860
In real life, Queen had several bass guitarists
before student of electronics John Deacon

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.130
met May and Taylor at a social event in early
1971 and shortly after successfully auditioned

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.130
for the band.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.780
Throughout Queen's history, Deacon has been
regarded as the quieter, more reserved bandmate,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.380
and he retired from the music business in
the late 1990s.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.390
In the movie, when Queen first meet their
new manager John Reid, he tells the band what

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.420
he can do for them, including getting them
on the radio, then maybe the TV show Top of

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.149
the Pops, and if that goes well, a promotional
tour of Japan.

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:52.140
Cut to the band singing Killer Queen on Top
of the Pops, followed by a montage of stage

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.920
performances which includes a little bit of
footage from the Japan tour.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.990
But Ben Hardy, who plays Roger Taylor, has
said there's an additional deleted scene set

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.409
in Japan which features the band playing the
song '39, and it was actually one of Hardy's

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:06.290
favourite days on set.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.790
'39, which appeared on Queen's fourth album
A Night At The Opera, tells the story of a

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:15.159
space traveller who goes on a year-long journey
but when he returns, discovers many more years

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:17.879
have actually passed and his loved ones have
died.

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:22.301
The song was written and sung on the album
by Brian May, but in live performances, Freddie

00:02:22.301 --> 00:02:23.710
usually led the vocals.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:28.530
After the charismatic frontman died, however,
Brian has often sung it as part of a tribute

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:33.060
to Freddie, and I suspect in Bohemian Rhapsody
the song was meant to foreshadow in some way

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.099
what would happen when Freddie passed away.

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:38.760
I imagine the song was eventually discarded
from the film though as there was still so

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.370
much to cover in Queen's story at this point.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.620
By the way, in reality, Queen had already
toured Japan and appeared on Top of the Pops

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:51.269
before John Reid became their manager in late
1975, though they did tour Japan again in

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:55.239
1976, this time under Reid's management as
part of their A Night At the Opera tour.

00:02:55.239 --> 00:02:59.980
Indeed, as you might expect of a movie, much
of Bohemian Rhapsody plays fast and loose

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.379
with Queen's real-life story and I talk more
about that in my other video; I'll link at

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:05.739
the end and in the video description.

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:10.680
A famous bit of Queen history that was cut
from the film was how Freddie Mercury composed

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.260
the catchy, Elvis tribute song Crazy Little
Thing Called Love in just 10 minutes on the

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.769
guitar at the same time as he was in the bath!

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:21.360
Rami Malek did shoot that scene, which sounds
like it would have been a fun little moment

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.960
in the movie, but it ended up getting the
chop.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.730
Based on the movie's soundtrack album, it
looks like the scene was meant to appear between

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.629
the tour scene featuring the song 'Now I'm
Here' and the scene where Freddie shows Mary

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:36.280
Austin video of the crowd singing 'Love Of
My Life' on the band's recent tour.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:40.350
Freddie Mercury's wilder side is alluded to
in Bohemian Rhapsody with several scenes of

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:44.230
his partying, however, the movie keeps things
strictly PG-13.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.299
According to Allen Leech, who plays Paul Prenter,
though, the snippets we see in the film of

00:03:48.299 --> 00:03:52.200
Freddie visiting the Meatpacking district
in New York, and specifically the gay bar

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.099
and sex club Mineshaft, were originally part
of a longer, separate scene which also continued

00:03:57.099 --> 00:03:58.430
outside the club.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:02.480
In the final film, clips of the Mineshaft
scene are intercut with scenes of the band

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.239
in the studio recording Another One Bites
The Dust.

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:09.611
The filmmakers haven't said why they cut this
scene, but Brian May and Roger Taylor were

00:04:09.611 --> 00:04:13.439
producers on the film and have spoken previously
about how they were keen for the movie in

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:17.780
general to really emphasise Freddie's musical
talent over other aspects of his life which

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.229
have so often been the focus of tabloid gossip.

00:04:20.229 --> 00:04:24.660
Lucy Boynton, who plays Mary Austin, has said
that several of the scenes she shares with

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.500
Malek's Freddie Mercury were trimmed in the
final film in order to keep the story moving

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.530
forward at a decent pace.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:34.560
Also cut from the Mary and Freddie scenes
is how Mercury came up with Queen's logo and

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.530
more on why he renamed the band from "Smile"
to "Queen."

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.330
There's a remnant of this moment in the final
film where Freddie and Mary talk about the

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.080
meaning of the new name "Queen", however,
what was chopped out is how Freddie came up

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.030
with the band's famous emblem.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:50.960
So we missed out on Freddie explaining that
Queen's logo is made up of the band members'

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.009
zodiac signs.

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:56.810
There's 2 lions, one for Roger Taylor and
another for John Deacon, who are both Leos;

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:02.080
a crab for Brian May's sign, Cancer; and two
fairies for Freddie's sign, Virgo.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.880
Freddie placed those together with the letter
Q which contained a crown, and topped it all

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.340
off with a Phoenix.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.840
While the need to keep up the pace of the
movie means casual fans might not realise

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.670
the emblem's origins, hardcore fans at least
will doubtless have noticed the brief glimpse

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:18.780
we got of Freddie's sketchbook with various
potential designs he was working on.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.169
Two of the movie's highlights are when the
band records Bohemian Rhapsody together in

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:27.639
the studio and the film's climax when they
perform an incredible set at Live Aid.

00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:31.980
What we hear in the movie during that big
on-stage sequence is Bohemian Rhapsody, Radio

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.259
Ga-Ga, an Ay-Oh crowd singalong, Hammer to
Fall, and We Are The Champions.

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:40.350
Now, if you're a big Queen fan, you'll know
that, in real life, the band also performed

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:44.539
another two songs at Wembley that day: Crazy
Little Thing Called Love and We Will Rock

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:45.539
You.

00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:49.920
And Rami Malek and his co-stars did actually
film the whole of that roughly 20-minute Live

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.980
Aid set, including those two songs.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:56.039
However, as the film was running long and
We Will Rock You had already appeared earlier,

00:05:56.039 --> 00:05:57.789
it didn't make the final cut.

00:05:57.789 --> 00:06:02.009
I suspect Crazy Little Thing Called Love was
cut from this part of the film for the same

00:06:02.009 --> 00:06:06.690
reason, however, because the filmmakers also
ended up cutting the scene where Mercury composes

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.930
that song, in the end, Crazy Little Thing
Called Love was missing from the movie.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:14.740
Interestingly, the Live Aid sequence was the
first thing they shot, so Malek and his fellow

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.520
actors were really thrown in at the deep end,
which makes it even more amazing that they

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:22.479
did such an outstanding job bringing that
massive event to life.

00:06:22.479 --> 00:06:26.789
And let's face it, Malek and co certainly
had a lot to live up to as Queen's performance

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:31.009
that day has frequently been labelled one
of the greatest live rock performances ever.

00:06:31.009 --> 00:06:34.910
I don't know about you, but I'd have happily
watched the cast perform both Crazy Little

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:39.190
Thing Called Love and We Will Rock You again
during that final sequence, so fingers crossed

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.520
that we get to see the full version of the
Live Aid gig on the Blu-ray.

